1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Monitoring supports assessment by making change visible. It becomes
unsafe when a displayed number is treated as more real than the
patient without checking signal quality and clinical context.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:27,024
First identify the measured variable: cuff pressure oscillations,
light absorption, glucose reaction or exhaled carbon dioxide. The
device may estimate a useful value without measuring the final
clinical outcome directly.

3
00:00:27,024 --> 00:00:38,472
Then ask what could distort it and whether it fits other findings. A
normal-looking saturation cannot settle a question about carbon
monoxide, ventilation or severe anemia.

4
00:00:38,472 --> 00:00:51,168
Act on an immediate threat while checking reliability. Treating the
patient first does not mean discarding inconvenient numbers; it means
investigating discrepancies without delaying necessary support.

5
00:00:51,168 --> 00:01:04,344
NEWS2 assigns points to six physiological observations and adds two
for supplemental oxygen. It helps recognize and communicate
deterioration risk; it does not identify the cause.

6
00:01:04,344 --> 00:01:15,912
Use a complete, contemporaneous set. An unmeasured temperature or
uncertain respiratory count must not be silently scored zero to
produce a reassuring total.

7
00:01:15,912 --> 00:01:29,376
The RCP tool is intended for adults aged sixteen and over, not
children under sixteen or pregnant patients. Use the relevant
pediatric or obstetric pathway rather than stretching this adult
score.

8
00:01:29,376 --> 00:01:42,840
Low NEWS2 cannot exclude major trauma, stroke, poisoning, evolving
respiratory failure or another serious condition. Clinical concern
remains a reason to escalate at any total.

9
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:55,992
For respiratory rate, eight or fewer scores three; nine to eleven
scores one; twelve to twenty scores zero; twenty-one to twenty-four
scores two; twenty-five or more scores three.

10
00:01:55,992 --> 00:02:09,336
On saturation scale one, ninety-one percent or below scores three,
ninety-two to ninety-three scores two, ninety-four to ninety-five
scores one, and ninety-six or above scores zero.

11
00:02:09,336 --> 00:02:21,240
If supplemental oxygen is being used, add two points separately. Do
not omit them because the saturation itself now looks normal, and do
not count both saturation scales.

12
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:32,352
These are score boundaries, not oxygen-treatment thresholds. Continue
indicated care and record the device and flow so another clinician can
interpret the observation.

13
00:02:32,352 --> 00:02:45,120
For systolic pressure, ninety or below scores three, ninety-one to one
hundred scores two, one hundred and one to one hundred and ten scores
one, and two hundred and twenty or above scores three.

14
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:56,832
The broad one-hundred-and-eleven to two-hundred-and-nineteen band
scores zero. The school sometimes calls it normal blood pressure; that
wording is misleading and must not become a clinical conclusion.

15
00:02:56,832 --> 00:03:07,848
Pulse scores three at forty or below or one hundred and thirty-one or
above. The remaining bands are shown here; use the exact boundary
rather than a rounded rate.

16
00:03:07,848 --> 00:03:20,904
For example, a pulse of one hundred and ten scores one, but one
hundred and eleven scores two. Recheck a questionable measurement; do
not alter the recorded value to fit a preferred score.

17
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:36,264
Temperature thirty-five or below scores three, thirty-five point one
to thirty-six scores one, thirty-six point one to thirty-eight scores
zero, thirty-eight point one to thirty-nine scores one, and thirty-
nine point one or above scores two.

18
00:03:36,264 --> 00:03:51,792
An alert patient scores zero for consciousness. New confusion or a
response only to voice or pressure, or no response, scores three.
Establish whether confusion is new rather than guessing from age or
diagnosis.

19
00:03:51,792 --> 00:04:03,504
ACVPU is the score's coarse responsiveness measure. Keep the detailed
eye, verbal and motor observations in the clinical record when
assessed; one does not replace the other.

20
00:04:03,504 --> 00:04:13,920
An absent fever score does not rule out infection. Hypothermia and a
mental-status change can be especially concerning in an older person
with possible sepsis.

21
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:35,040
RCP scale two is for a clinically selected hypercapnic-respiratory-
failure context with a target of eighty-eight to ninety-two percent.
Older BCEHS A02 instead states diagnosed COPD. The school body uses
both formulations; document the applicable service instruction and
seek clarification if uncertain.

22
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:47,928
For low saturation on this scale, eighty-three or below scores three,
eighty-four to eighty-five scores two and eighty-six to eighty-seven
scores one. Eighty-eight to ninety-two scores zero.

23
00:04:47,928 --> 00:05:05,136
Above ninety-two, the oxygen context matters: on room air, ninety-
three or above scores zero. On oxygen, ninety-three to ninety-four
scores one, ninety-five to ninety-six scores two and ninety-seven or
above scores three.

24
00:05:05,136 --> 00:05:18,720
Add the separate two oxygen points whenever oxygen is used. Do not
confuse this scoring scheme with A07's chronic-hypoxemia treatment
band or apply it to make an acute hypoxic patient look low risk.

25
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:32,280
This original example has respiratory rate twenty-three and saturation
ninety-four percent on supplemental oxygen, using scale one. Those
contribute two, one and two points respectively.

26
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:44,472
Systolic pressure one hundred and six contributes one, pulse one
hundred and fourteen contributes two, temperature thirty-eight point
four contributes one, and alertness contributes zero.

27
00:05:44,472 --> 00:05:56,832
The sum is nine, not seven. Omitting supplemental oxygen would
understate risk. Retain the component values so the total is auditable
and another clinician can see what drove it.

28
00:05:56,832 --> 00:06:10,536
Reassess immediate threats, escalate to the appropriate clinical
support, increase monitoring and organize urgent conveyance and
notification. The score supports urgency but does not diagnose the
illness.

29
00:06:10,536 --> 00:06:23,232
A patient with otherwise zero-scoring observations but new confusion
has a single red parameter of three. Calling the aggregate low without
recognizing that red flag misses the point of the tool.

30
00:06:23,232 --> 00:06:37,152
The original RCP framework treats a single red three as needing urgent
clinical review, distinct from the aggregate five-to-six and seven-
plus pathways. Do not describe every red flag as the same response
tier.

31
00:06:37,152 --> 00:06:51,840
Check that the finding is new and assess the cause. Ask about
baseline, last-known-well and associated symptoms while evaluating
immediate oxygenation, ventilation, perfusion and glucose threats.

32
00:06:51,840 --> 00:07:02,832
A red flag does not require waiting for another parameter to worsen
before seeking help. Continue treatment and urgency decisions from the
patient as well as the score.

33
00:07:02,832 --> 00:07:16,464
Learn the aggregate bands: zero to four, five to six, and seven or
more, while checking for an individual three. A rising total also
deserves attention even before a band changes.

34
00:07:16,464 --> 00:07:31,608
The school calls for urgent review and increasing monitoring as risk
rises, and uses medical-direction consultation in its high-risk
teaching. The actual service consultation and notification route must
be verified for the patient and pathway.

35
00:07:31,608 --> 00:07:43,824
RCP response charts were designed around organized hospital teams. A
four-hour low-score observation schedule is not permission to leave an
ambulance patient unchecked for four hours.

36
00:07:43,824 --> 00:07:55,488
Report measured components, oxygen, total, trend, treatment and the
help requested. An urgent message must say what the patient needs, not
only announce a score.

37
00:07:55,488 --> 00:08:12,288
The new BCEHS documentation section fifteen does not exactly match the
older A01 and A02 wording. It describes thirty-minute observations
only for selected untreated patients without altered vital signs or
listed complaints.

38
00:08:12,288 --> 00:08:27,504
It specifies fifteen-minute intervals for symptomatic or abnormal
patients and more frequent checks when indicated; critical shock may
need observations every two to three minutes. Before-and-after
treatment observations remain important.

39
00:08:27,504 --> 00:08:41,280
It also says NEWS2 is required by certain ASTaR pathways, whereas
older A02 says all patients. This is a genuine publication difference,
not a reason to pretend one page says something else.

40
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:57,720
For school preparation, perform the assigned NEWS2 and five- or
fifteen-minute practice as requested. For operations, confirm the
applicable instruction with the service; do not use this conflict to
reduce monitoring of an unwell patient.

41
00:08:57,720 --> 00:09:10,872
Carbon monoxide is not reliably detected by human smell or sight.
Several occupants with similar symptoms, a generator or heater, or an
alarm should trigger a shared-exposure concern.

42
00:09:10,872 --> 00:09:22,560
Do not enter an unsafe area to attach a monitor. Activate the
appropriate hazard-control and rescue resources, and have patients
removed to a safe environment by trained personnel.

43
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:35,904
A building detector measures environmental concentration, often parts
per million. A patient CO reading estimates a hemoglobin fraction.
Confusing these units can create a dangerous decision.

44
00:09:35,904 --> 00:09:47,856
BCEHS J01 prohibits care in hot or warm zones. The school mentions
SCBA, but owning or naming that equipment does not authorize a
paramedic to enter a hazardous zone.

45
00:09:47,856 --> 00:10:00,120
Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin much more avidly than oxygen. It
reduces available oxygen-carrying capacity, and makes the remaining
oxygen harder to release to tissues.

46
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:12,144
CO also affects proteins and cellular respiration, contributing to
tissue dysfunction beyond what a saturation number can reveal. The
brain and heart are particularly vulnerable.

47
00:10:12,144 --> 00:10:27,360
The school calls the left-shifted oxygen-release effect the Haldane
effect. That label is inaccurate here: the Haldane effect concerns
oxygenation changing blood carbon-dioxide carriage. Learn the CO
mechanism without that mistaken name.

48
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:41,712
Headache and nausea may progress to confusion, syncope, chest pain,
seizures or collapse. Do not require a dramatic skin colour or a
particular meter percentage before recognizing possible poisoning.

49
00:10:41,712 --> 00:10:53,520
A conventional two-wavelength oximeter can misread carbon-monoxide-
bound hemoglobin and produce a falsely reassuring saturation. It is
not a CO screening or exclusion test.

50
00:10:53,520 --> 00:11:07,416
A multiwavelength pulse CO-oximeter estimates SpCO noninvasively.
Blood laboratory CO-oximetry measures COHb using a blood sample. The
two are not interchangeable gold standards.

51
00:11:07,416 --> 00:11:23,016
BCEHS J02 warns that point-of-care CO readings can be unreliable and
that a raised COHb may not reflect poisoning severity or delayed
neurological risk. A low reading after oxygen does not erase the
earlier exposure.

52
00:11:23,016 --> 00:11:34,944
The school gives smoker and symptom-percentage bands. Learn that
smoking can elevate baseline, but do not use those bands as a
deterministic symptom chart or a safe discharge rule.

53
00:11:34,944 --> 00:11:49,176
Once safe, provide the indicated high-flow oxygen and supportive care.
Ordinary SpO2 is not a reason to withhold oxygen in suspected CO
poisoning. Support ventilation if it is inadequate.

54
00:11:49,176 --> 00:12:03,552
Reassess neurological state, symptoms, perfusion and relevant cardiac
findings. Record exposure circumstances, removal time, oxygen timing
and measured values with the instrument identified.

55
00:12:03,552 --> 00:12:15,648
Current J02 requires CliniCall care-planning consultation for every
suspected or confirmed CO case, not only a severe case or a value
above twenty-five percent.

56
00:12:15,648 --> 00:12:30,384
A severe clinical presentation, pregnancy or significant cardiac or
neurological effects informs specialist and hyperbaric discussion. Do
not make a destination or no-conveyance decision from one noninvasive
percentage alone.

57
00:12:30,384 --> 00:12:43,584
A meter reading is generated by a strip reaction. Sugar on the skin, a
wet sampling site, insufficient blood, expired strips and temperature
extremes can make the displayed value misleading.

58
00:12:43,584 --> 00:12:54,096
Poor peripheral circulation can make a capillary sample less
representative. Reassess the patient and improve sampling where
possible while urgent care continues.

59
00:12:54,096 --> 00:13:05,568
If considering a venous or another sample, confirm that the meter and
local procedure permit it. Do not assume that any available blood can
be used in every point-of-care device.

60
00:13:05,568 --> 00:13:18,000
A result labelled low or high beyond the meter range is not an exact
number. Document the actual message, pursue an appropriate reliable
assessment and respond to the clinical threat.

61
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:31,320
Glucose below four millimoles per litre is hypoglycemia in the local
pathway. Check immediately with altered consciousness or seizure while
continuing airway, breathing and circulation assessment.

62
00:13:31,320 --> 00:13:40,872
A patient who follows commands and swallows safely may receive the
indicated oral correction. Do not place food or a drink into an
unprotected airway.

63
00:13:40,872 --> 00:13:54,120
If oral treatment is unsafe, use the authorized clinical pathway:
intravenous dextrose when appropriate, with an alternative such as
glucagon when access is unattainable according to the relevant
instruction.

64
00:13:54,120 --> 00:14:08,712
The brain is highly vulnerable to low glucose, but the school's claim
that glucose is its only possible fuel is too absolute. The practical
priority is rapid recognition and safe correction, followed by cause
assessment.

65
00:14:08,712 --> 00:14:22,920
D10W contains ten grams per hundred millilitres. That is point one
gram per millilitre, so fifteen grams corresponds to one hundred and
fifty millilitres. Keep grams and milligrams consistent.

66
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:36,672
The current adult BCEHS monograph lists ten to twenty-five grams
intravenously, equivalent to one hundred to two hundred and fifty
millilitres. Use the appropriate authorized plan and reassess the
clinical effect.

67
00:14:36,672 --> 00:14:56,352
The public PCP pediatric line explicitly states over twelve years:
five millilitres per kilogram of D10W, the stated adult maximum, over
five to fifteen minutes, and a repeat once. Do not silently extend it
to younger ages or settle the exact age boundary by guessing.

68
00:14:56,352 --> 00:15:10,872
Confirm a patent line before and during administration. Pain, swelling
or a change at the site can indicate extravasation with tissue injury;
stop and manage a suspected complication under the appropriate
procedure.

69
00:15:10,872 --> 00:15:25,512
Retest glucose and reassess the patient after correction and as
clinically indicated. The school suggests five-to-ten-minute checks
during this process; deterioration requires an immediate check, not
waiting for a timer.

70
00:15:25,512 --> 00:15:36,096
Ask what caused the episode and whether it can recur. Long-acting
medication, inadequate intake or concurrent infection can make a brief
improvement misleading.

71
00:15:36,096 --> 00:15:55,824
Hypoglycemia can mimic or coexist with stroke. Current E01 calls for
immediate correction; the dextrose monograph recommends consultation
about care planning if intracerebral hemorrhage is suspected. That is
not a blanket command to delay every low-glucose treatment until a
stroke call finishes.

72
00:15:55,824 --> 00:16:11,424
Persistent confusion or focal deficit after correction requires
continued investigation and appropriate conveyance. Non-conveyance has
additional criteria, capacity and safety-planning requirements, not
just a glucose above four.

73
00:16:11,424 --> 00:16:22,920
An oscillometric monitor detects pressure oscillations in the cuff
during its measurement cycle. Algorithms estimate arterial pressure
from the signal; they do not listen to Korotkoff sounds.

74
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:35,712
MAP relates to the oscillation envelope and systolic and diastolic
values are algorithm-dependent. This does not make every displayed MAP
accurate or guarantee it is the best clinical measure in shock.

75
00:16:35,712 --> 00:16:48,456
Cuff size and height, movement, shivering, arrhythmia and low
perfusion can impair results. A monitor that cycles repeatedly may be
failing to obtain a trustworthy signal.

76
00:16:48,456 --> 00:16:59,880
Check the patient and setup, then repeat or confirm with manual
measurement when appropriate. Record method and relevant limitations
instead of choosing the most comfortable number.

77
00:16:59,880 --> 00:17:08,520
A low pressure obtained during shivering might be distorted. But new
drowsiness and cool skin make it unsafe to call the whole event
artifact.

78
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:21,624
Reassess immediate threats, pulse, breathing and relevant causes while
a partner checks cuff size, position and signal conditions. Do not
stop urgent support to repeat the same poor technique.

79
00:17:21,624 --> 00:17:33,696
Obtain a better measurement as feasible and compare timed
observations. A confirmed downward trend supports escalation even if a
later reading has not crossed a familiar threshold.

80
00:17:33,696 --> 00:17:46,824
An extremely high pressure likewise needs confirmation and symptom
assessment. A number alone neither proves a hypertensive emergency nor
justifies an unsupervised pressure-lowering intervention.

81
00:17:46,824 --> 00:18:00,024
If ventilation rises while production and circulation are stable, end-
tidal carbon dioxide tends to fall. But a low number can also reflect
reduced blood flow carrying carbon dioxide to the lungs.

82
00:18:00,024 --> 00:18:13,152
For the same reason, very poor ventilation does not always produce a
tall high waveform: a tiny sampled breath or severe low flow can give
a low or absent signal despite dangerous respiratory depression.

83
00:18:13,152 --> 00:18:25,848
A high value may reflect hypoventilation, increased carbon-dioxide
delivery or rebreathing, depending on context. Interpret number,
shape, breathing and perfusion together.

84
00:18:25,848 --> 00:18:35,880
Do not change a patient's breathing simply to force a number into a
reference band. First identify the likely physiology and any
measurement problem.

85
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:50,688
Identify the sequence on an actual waveform: baseline, expiratory
upstroke, alveolar plateau and inspiratory downstroke. Phase naming
differs between teaching conventions, but the gas sequence does not.

86
00:18:50,688 --> 00:19:03,216
The alpha angle lies between the upstroke and plateau, and the beta
angle between plateau and downstroke. The school gives roughly one
hundred to one hundred and ten degrees and ninety degrees
respectively.

87
00:19:03,216 --> 00:19:15,864
Screen scaling and physiology affect the apparent geometry. Use slope,
baseline, duration and serial change as clues rather than treating a
measured angle as an independent diagnosis.

88
00:19:15,864 --> 00:19:27,600
Confirm that the sample is actually exhaled gas from the patient. A
plateau without the expected clinical breath, or a line blocked by
water, should prompt a sampling check.

89
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:41,928
An obstructive shark-fin shape has a sloped rise and plateau. It
supports impaired expiratory emptying; assess air entry, effort and
fatigue, and follow the relevant airway or respiratory plan.

90
00:19:41,928 --> 00:19:54,264
Tall widely spaced breaths can fit hypoventilation, while low rapid
breaths can fit increased ventilation. Neither description excludes
poor perfusion or unreliable sampling.

91
00:19:54,264 --> 00:20:07,584
Low carbon dioxide with deep rapid breathing may be compensation for
metabolic acidosis. Do not label it panic before considering dangerous
metabolic, respiratory or circulatory causes.

92
00:20:07,584 --> 00:20:21,960
If the baseline does not return toward zero, inspect the patient,
circuit, valves, gas flow and sampling as applicable to the actual
device. Do not replace a random filter without identifying the system.

93
00:20:21,960 --> 00:20:36,240
If the waveform suddenly disappears, look at the patient immediately
while checking the circuit and sampling connection. A disconnected
line and a respiratory arrest require different corrections, but both
need prompt recognition.

94
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:48,864
A sudden sustained rise during resuscitation may indicate return of
circulation. Confirm through the appropriate clinical rhythm and
circulation check without creating an unnecessary prolonged pause.

95
00:20:48,864 --> 00:21:02,304
A falling value over several readings can reflect deteriorating
perfusion, but also changes in ventilation or sampling. Compare pulse,
pressure, mentation and the actual breath.

96
00:21:02,304 --> 00:21:15,816
The school references an end-tidal value above ten during CPR. Treat a
poor reading as a reason to assess quality and physiology, not as a
stand-alone survival prediction or termination criterion.

97
00:21:15,816 --> 00:21:29,616
A cleft in the plateau, historically called a curare cleft, can
reflect spontaneous effort during controlled ventilation or patient-
ventilator interaction. It does not by itself prove a need for more
sedation.

98
00:21:29,616 --> 00:21:41,856
Small regular oscillations related to cardiac activity may appear on
the trace. Correlate their timing with the pulse and the patient
before interpreting them as separate breaths or a major fault.

99
00:21:41,856 --> 00:21:52,992
If there is distress, inadequate breath delivery or suspected
equipment trouble, assess immediately and involve the clinician
trained to manage the airway and ventilator.

100
00:21:52,992 --> 00:22:04,800
The school includes these patterns as recognition knowledge. Learning
the name does not authorize a student to alter ventilator settings,
neuromuscular blockade or sedation independently.

101
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:17,592
Consider a drowsy patient with a normal saturation on oxygen, a weak
respiratory effort and an abnormal capnogram. Saturation alone must
not hide inadequate ventilation.

102
00:22:17,592 --> 00:22:30,048
In another patient, headache affects the whole household despite high
ordinary saturation. The exposure pattern changes safety and care
priorities before a patient CO instrument is available.

103
00:22:30,048 --> 00:22:42,024
For every case, state which evidence supports your concern and what is
still uncertain. Seek the relevant clinical help, not a more
impressive label unsupported by assessment.

104
00:22:42,024 --> 00:22:54,888
Record the findings, instrument and context, action, response and
reassessment time. The next session applies the same reasoning to
medication selection and manual infusion calculations.
